<section class="sec" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>How the AI website generator works</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">It's prompt to website, with no template to choose and nothing to set up first. You start with words, and the generator takes care of layout, copy and pictures from there.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>01</b><h3>Write a prompt</h3><p>Say who you are, what you offer and who the site is for. Would you rather show than tell? Upload a screenshot, a wireframe or a sketch on paper instead.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><b>02</b><h3>Compare four drafts</h3><p>Each draft takes its own direction in layout, palette and typography. Nothing fits? Reword the prompt or add detail and run it again.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><b>03</b><h3>Refine the winner</h3><p>Type requests like "swap the photos for something warmer", or switch to the block editor and click into any heading, image or button.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><b>04</b><h3>Go live</h3><p>Publish on a free subdomain with SSL, point your own domain at it, or send the files to the host you already use.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="tips" id="tips">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>What to tell the AI</h2><a class="btn" href="#top" data-go>Start writing</a></div>
    <p class="lede">The generator works with whatever you type, but specifics produce a stronger first draft. These are the details that change the result the most.</p>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div><h3>The name and kind of business</h3><p>"Hartley &amp; Sons, a family bakery" gives the AI far more to work with than "a bakery". The name shows up in the header, the headings and the page title.</p></div>
      <div><h3>What you sell or do</h3><p>List your main services or products. Each one can turn into its own block with a heading, a short description and a picture.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Who your visitors are</h3><p>Students, busy parents, hotel guests or procurement managers: the audience shapes the wording, the imagery and how formal the site sounds.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Where you work</h3><p>A city or neighborhood makes the copy local and gives your address, opening hours and service area an obvious place on the page.</p></div>
      <div><h3>The mood</h3><p>Words like calm, bold, playful, premium or minimal steer the colors, fonts and photo style of all four drafts.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Your brand colors</h3><p>If you already have colors, mention them in plain words or as hex codes, so the palette starts from them rather than from a guess.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Sections you need</h3><p>Ask for a price list, a menu, a team section, an FAQ or a booking form, and it's in the first draft instead of something you add later.</p></div>
      <div><h3>One page or several</h3><p>Say whether you want a single scrolling landing page or separate pages for services, about and contact.</p></div>
      <div><h3>What visitors should do</h3><p>Call, book, request a quote, buy, subscribe. Naming the goal puts the right buttons and forms where people will look for them.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Facts only you know</h3><p>Years in business, prices, guarantees, awards. Real numbers make the copy specific instead of generic.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Your language</h3><p>Write the prompt in the language your visitors read, and the site text is generated in that language from the start.</p></div>
      <div><h3>A picture of what you like</h3><p>A screenshot of a site you admire or a rough pencil sketch explains a layout faster than a paragraph of description.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="edit">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Change anything after it's generated</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">A draft is a starting point. Keep reshaping it with words, with the mouse, or both, switching whenever it suits the change you're making.</p>
    <div class="ways">
      <div class="way w-chat"><h3>Ask in chat</h3><p>Type an instruction the way you'd brief a designer: "put the prices in a table", "make it feel more premium", "add a section about our warranty". The page updates in front of you.</p></div>
      <div class="way w-hand"><h3>Edit by hand</h3><p>Open the same page in the visual editor. Click a heading to retype it, drag a block to a new spot, replace a photo, or open a block's settings for spacing, colors and layout.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="roles">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Three jobs the AI takes off your hands</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">A small website usually needs a designer, a copywriter and a developer. Mobirise AI covers the routine part of each role, so you only step in where your own judgment matters.</p>
    <div class="roles">
      <div class="role r-design">
        <h3>The designer</h3>
        <p>Decides how the site looks, from the first draft to a complete restyle months later, and keeps every page consistent.</p>
        <h4>Current styles</h4><p>Drafts follow how sites look today: generous white space, frosted-glass panels, soft color gradients. Nothing feels like a layout from ten years ago.</p>
        <h4>Pictures made to order</h4><p>Rather than reusing the stock photos everyone has seen, the AI creates images that fit the subject of each block and the colors around it.</p>
        <h4>Short videos</h4><p>For a hero section or a promo banner, it can put together a brief clip with captions and a music track.</p>
        <h4>A new look in one step</h4><p>Tired of the design? Ask for another theme, palette or font pairing, and your text and images move into it untouched.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="role r-copy">
        <h3>The copywriter</h3>
        <p>Writes and polishes every word your visitors read, in whichever language they speak.</p>
        <h4>Text that fits your field</h4><p>A dental clinic and a skate shop don't sound alike. Headings, offers and button labels are written in a voice that suits the trade and the brand.</p>
        <h4>Clearer, shorter, warmer</h4><p>Pick a paragraph and ask for a plainer, friendlier or half-length version. The AI also flags passages that don't help visitors find what they came for.</p>
        <h4>Translation</h4><p>Neural translation turns one section or every page into other languages, so a single project can serve visitors in several countries.</p>
        <h4>Advice as you go</h4><p>While you edit, an assistant points out weak spots in structure, color pairs and wording, and suggests how to fix them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="role r-dev">
        <h3>The developer</h3>
        <p>Takes care of the technical side you would otherwise hire someone for.</p>
        <h4>Standards-based code</h4><p>Pages are built from valid HTML, CSS and JavaScript that adapt to every screen size and stay yours to download.</p>
        <h4>SEO groundwork</h4><p>Content structure and metadata are prepared during generation, ready for search engines and for the AI tools that answer questions from the web.</p>
        <h4>Forms that sort themselves</h4><p>Contact forms can group incoming messages by topic and add a two-line summary to each inquiry, so urgent ones stand out.</p>
        <h4>A shop that works</h4><p>Product listings, a cart and a secure checkout are generated together, so a store can take orders from the very first draft.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Why start from a generated draft</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">A template hands you someone else's site with gaps to fill. A generated draft is already about you, and that changes how the rest of the work goes.</p>
    <div class="uses">
      <div class="use"><h3>No placeholder text</h3><p>Templates arrive full of lorem ipsum and stock photos to replace. A generated draft already describes your business, so you edit instead of writing from scratch.</p></div>
      <div class="use"><h3>Options you wouldn't try</h3><p>Four directions side by side make choosing easier, and one of them is often a layout you'd never have picked from a catalog.</p></div>
      <div class="use"><h3>A first text to react to</h3><p>Headlines, offers and button wording are written from your prompt. Reacting to a decent draft is much faster than facing an empty page.</p></div>
      <div class="use"><h3>Changes in plain words</h3><p>You don't need to know which panel holds which setting. Describe the result you want, and the AI finds the block and the option.</p></div>
      <div class="use"><h3>Real blocks, not a picture</h3><p>Every section is a regular Mobirise block. Move it, restyle it, duplicate it or delete it, exactly as you would on a site you built by hand.</p></div>
 